      "content": "In the Phoenix metropolitan area, the asphalt pavement is subjected to extreme diurnal temperature fluctuations, which have a significant impact on the rheological properties of bitumen. During the day, the high temperatures can cause the bitumen to reach a state of high fluidity. This fluid state allows the bitumen to flow and deform under the weight of traffic, which can lead to rutting and permanent deformation of the pavement surface. The rheological property that is most affected during this phase is the viscosity of the bitumen, which decreases as temperature increases.\n\nAs temperatures drop at night, the bitumen transitions to a more brittle state. The viscosity increases, and the bitumen becomes less able to deform under stress. This increased brittleness can lead to the formation of transverse and longitudinal cracks as the pavement is subjected to the tensile stresses of traffic loads. The tensile stress within the asphalt matrix is directly related to the stiffness of the bitumen; as the bitumen becomes more brittle, the pavement becomes more susceptible to cracking.\n\nThe subgrade, which is the foundation of the pavement, also experiences thermal expansion and contraction due to the diurnal temperature fluctuations. If the subgrade is not properly stabilized, this can lead to additional stresses within the pavement structure, exacerbating",
